@star mira
name: Mira variable
makeup: hydrogen 90.5%, helium 9.4%, oxygen 0.045%, nitrogen 0.02%, carbon 0.012%, neon 0.008%, magnesium 0.004%, silicon 0.003%, iron 0.003%, titanium 0.0001%
surface-kind: giant
real: yes
says: a pulsing red giant that brightens and dims over months
radius-sol: 200..400
temp-k: 2500..3500
share-of-stars: rare

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule star-color
real: rule
says: color follows surface temperature, from red (coolest) to blue (hottest)

@rule star-life
real: rule
says: heavier stars burn hotter and die sooner, lighter stars burn cooler and live longer

@rule star-pick
real: rule
says: when a galaxy is generated, red dwarfs are by far the most common and blue stars the rarest

@rule star-vary
real: rule
says: every generated star picks its values from inside its type's ranges, from the world seed

@rule star-name
real: rule
says: our own star is never named here; every star is a star

# ── A star's layers (from its surface down to its core) ──

# from-r / to-r: the layer's reach as a share of the star's radius from its middle (1 the surface); matter: plasma (bare
#   nuclei and free electrons at the temperature and density given); temp-k and density-g-cc run from the layer's top to
#   its bottom; makeup: what it is made of by count of atoms (the star's own surface make-up where none is given); in: the
#   kinds of star that have it (none: every shining star of the main sequence). The layers follow the standard model of a
#   star of one solar mass (Bahcall et al. 2005), the convective cores and radiative envelopes of heavier stars, the
#   fully convective red dwarfs, the burning shells round a giant's helium core, the onion shells of a supergiant down to
#   its iron core (Woosley, Heger and Weaver 2002) and the carbon-oxygen of a white dwarf.
@layer photosphere
name: Photosphere
from-r: 0.9985
to-r: 1
matter: plasma
temp-k: 5800..6500
density-g-cc: 2e-7..1e-6
real: yes
says: the face we see: its light leaves from here, with the granules of rising and sinking plasma
in: o-type, b-type, a-type, f-type, g-type, k-type, m-type, protostar, t-tauri, wolf-rayet, cepheid, rr-lyrae, red-giant, red-supergiant, blue-supergiant, carbon-star, s-type, mira

@layer convective-zone
name: Convective zone
from-r: 0.71
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 6500..2.0e6
density-g-cc: 1e-6..0.2
real: yes
says: churning: hot plasma rising, cooled plasma sinking, carrying the heat out the last third of the way
in: f-type, g-type, k-type, protostar, t-tauri, cepheid, rr-lyrae

@layer radiative-zone
name: Radiative zone
from-r: 0.25
to-r: 0.71
matter: plasma
temp-k: 2.0e6..7.0e6
density-g-cc: 0.2..20
real: yes
says: the heat crawls out as light, scattered so often that it takes about a hundred thousand years to cross
in: f-type, g-type, k-type, protostar, t-tauri, cepheid, rr-lyrae

@layer core
name: Core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.25
matter: plasma
temp-k: 7.0e6..1.57e7
density-g-cc: 20..150
makeup: hydrogen 66%, helium 34%
real: yes
says: fusion: hydrogen into helium, about four million tonnes of mass turned to light each second in a star of one solar mass; the helium made over its life gathers here
in: f-type, g-type, k-type, protostar, t-tauri, cepheid, rr-lyrae

@layer radiative-envelope
name: Radiative envelope
from-r: 0.25
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 1.0e4..1.0e7
density-g-cc: 1e-7..10
real: yes
says: in a heavy star the heat climbs out as light the whole way, no churning until the core
in: o-type, b-type, a-type, wolf-rayet

@layer convective-core
name: Convective core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.25
matter: plasma
temp-k: 1.0e7..3.5e7
density-g-cc: 10..40
makeup: hydrogen 60%, helium 40%
real: yes
says: a heavy star burns hydrogen so fiercely that its core churns, by the CNO cycle: carbon, nitrogen and oxygen passing the protons along
in: o-type, b-type, a-type, wolf-rayet

@layer convective-all
name: Convective interior
from-r: 0.12
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 3500..6.0e6
density-g-cc: 1e-6..60
real: yes
says: a red dwarf churns from its surface almost to its core, so all its hydrogen gets its turn in the fire: it burns for trillions of years
in: m-type

@layer dwarf-core
name: Core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.12
matter: plasma
temp-k: 6.0e6..8.0e6
density-g-cc: 60..100
makeup: hydrogen 85%, helium 15%
real: yes
says: slow fusion, hydrogen into helium, at the lowest heat that can keep it going
in: m-type

@layer giant-envelope
name: Envelope
from-r: 0.08
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 3500..5.0e6
density-g-cc: 1e-9..0.01
real: yes
says: a vast thin churning envelope of hydrogen, swollen a hundredfold when the core ran out of hydrogen
in: red-giant, carbon-star, s-type, mira

@layer hydrogen-shell
name: Hydrogen-burning shell
from-r: 0.03
to-r: 0.08
matter: plasma
temp-k: 2.0e7..5.0e7
density-g-cc: 0.01..1e3
makeup: hydrogen 50%, helium 50%
real: yes
says: hydrogen burning in a shell round the spent core, the heat that puffs the envelope out
in: red-giant, carbon-star, s-type, mira

@layer helium-core
name: Helium core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.03
matter: plasma
temp-k: 5.0e7..1.2e8
density-g-cc: 1e3..1e5
makeup: helium 70%, carbon 20%, oxygen 10%
real: yes
says: the helium the star made over its life, pressed to the density of a white dwarf, burning into carbon and oxygen
in: red-giant, carbon-star, s-type, mira

@layer supergiant-envelope
name: Hydrogen envelope
from-r: 0.3
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 3500..5.0e6
density-g-cc: 1e-9..0.1
real: yes
says: mostly hydrogen still, the outer bulk of a star that has burned through heavier and heavier fuels inside
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer helium-shell
name: Helium shell
from-r: 0.2
to-r: 0.3
matter: plasma
temp-k: 5.0e6..2.0e8
density-g-cc: 0.1..1e3
makeup: helium 90%, carbon 7%, oxygen 3%
real: yes
says: helium, the ash of hydrogen, burning into carbon and oxygen
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer carbon-oxygen-shell
name: Carbon-oxygen shell
from-r: 0.12
to-r: 0.2
matter: plasma
temp-k: 2.0e8..8.0e8
density-g-cc: 1e3..1e5
makeup: carbon 45%, oxygen 45%, neon 10%
real: yes
says: carbon burning into neon and magnesium
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer neon-magnesium-shell
name: Neon-magnesium shell
from-r: 0.08
to-r: 0.12
matter: plasma
temp-k: 8.0e8..1.5e9
density-g-cc: 1e5..1e6
makeup: oxygen 50%, neon 30%, magnesium 20%
real: yes
says: neon burning into oxygen and magnesium, a stage that lasts about a year
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer oxygen-silicon-shell
name: Oxygen-silicon shell
from-r: 0.04
to-r: 0.08
matter: plasma
temp-k: 1.5e9..2.5e9
density-g-cc: 1e6..1e7
makeup: oxygen 40%, silicon 35%, sulfur 25%
real: yes
says: oxygen burning into silicon and sulfur, in months
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer silicon-shell
name: Silicon shell
from-r: 0.02
to-r: 0.04
matter: plasma
temp-k: 2.5e9..3.5e9
density-g-cc: 1e7..5e7
makeup: silicon 40%, sulfur 30%, argon 15%, calcium 15%
real: yes
says: silicon burning into iron and nickel, the last fuel, spent in about a day
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer iron-core
name: Iron core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.02
matter: plasma
temp-k: 3.5e9..1.0e10
density-g-cc: 5e7..1e10
makeup: iron 70%, nickel 20%, chromium 5%, titanium 5%
real: yes
says: iron, the end of the line: fusing it takes energy instead of giving it; when the core grows past 1.4 solar masses it collapses in a second, and the star goes supernova
in: red-supergiant, blue-supergiant

@layer white-dwarf-air
name: Atmosphere
from-r: 0.999
to-r: 1
matter: plasma
temp-k: 1.0e4..5.0e4
density-g-cc: 1e-6..1
makeup: hydrogen 99%, helium 1%
real: yes
says: a thin skin of hydrogen or helium, the only thing left of the star's outer layers
in: white-dwarf

@layer white-dwarf-body
name: Degenerate body
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.999
matter: plasma
temp-k: 5.0e4..1.0e7
density-g-cc: 1..1e6
makeup: carbon 50%, oxygen 50%
real: yes
says: carbon and oxygen pressed to a tonne a cubic centimetre, held up by electrons squeezed as close as they can go; it burns nothing, only cools, for billions of years
in: white-dwarf

@layer brown-dwarf-body
name: Interior
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.9985
matter: plasma
temp-k: 2000..1.0e6
density-g-cc: 1e-5..100
real: yes
says: hydrogen and helium pressed to a metal, churning; too light ever to burn hydrogen, only deuterium for a while
in: brown-dwarf, l-dwarf, t-dwarf, y-dwarf

